{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed262781723/15/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"78e78cff\" lines=\"71-91\">\n<description>\nRecolor the row-wise passage indicated by wall pixels with the singleton marker color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background contains one singleton marker pixel and one wall color.\n- Wall pixels lie on several rows and form left/right limits around the marker column.\n- Empty rows between the top and bottom wall rows are open horizontal passages.\n\nRule:\n- On a wall row, paint background pixels between the nearest wall strictly\n  left of the marker column and the nearest wall at or right of it; if only\n  one side is present, paint from that wall to the opposite grid edge.\n- On empty rows between the top and bottom wall rows, paint the whole row.\n- On empty rows outside the wall-row band, reuse the nearest end wall-row interval.\n- Preserve wall pixels and the original marker pixel.\n\nKey insight:\n- The wall color defines scanline limits relative to the marker column; the\n  marker color fills those implied horizontal spans rather than flood-filling\n  through connected background.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1478ab18\" lines=\"35-43\">\n<description>\nPaint the teal outline of the right triangle determined by the missing corner of the grey-marker square.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid is an orange background with exactly four foreground marker pixels of one color. The markers have a square bounding box: three markers are bbox corners, and the fourth marker is inside that square.\nRule\nFind the one corner of the marker bounding square that has no marker. That missing corner is the right-angle vertex of a right isosceles triangle; paint its two axis-aligned legs and the opposite diagonal in teal, leaving existing marker pixels unchanged.\nKey insight\nThe present corner marker opposite the missing corner is a decoy; the three triangle vertices are the missing corner and the two marker corners adjacent to it.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e9fc42f2\" lines=\"26-43\">\n<description>\nAssemble the foreground objects by docking equal-colored boundary markers edge-to-edge.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color and several 4-connected multicolor foreground\nobjects.  One foreground color is the body color; the less frequent foreground colors are\nboundary connector markers, and each marker color appears once as a free end or twice as a\nmatched docking pair.\n\nRule\nCrop each foreground object to its bounding box and translate the objects so that the two\noccurrences of every paired connector color touch on their exposed boundary sides.  Preserve\neach object's orientation and colors, then return the tight crop around the assembled\nforeground on the original background color.\n\nKey insight\nThe colored marker pixels are ports: matching colors specify which objects must touch, and\nthe marker's side of its object determines the direction of the translation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"15660dd6\" lines=\"33-54\">\n<description>\nCombine each column's repeated template mask with its single solid color swatch.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is a table of framed panels separated by background rows/columns.\n- The leftmost column gives one solid row-label color for each panel row.\n- In each panel column, exactly one panel has a solid non-background interior,\n  while the other panels share the same mask drawn in one template color on\n  the background.\n\nRule:\n- For every panel column, find the row containing the solid interior swatch.\n- Rebuild one output panel using that row's left label as the frame color.\n- Fill the repeated template mask with the swatch color and leave the mask\n  background cells as background; separate rebuilt panels with one\n  background column.\n\nKey insight:\nThe table encodes a colorized stencil per column: the solid panel supplies\nthe paint color and frame label, while the duplicate template panels supply\nthe shape mask.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"db7260a4\" lines=\"37-56\">\n<description>\nDrop the singleton marker into the matching red U-shaped container, or make a bottom-row puddle if no closed container is hit.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background, one singleton marker pixel on the top row,\n  and one other foreground color forming vertical bars and bottom segments.\n- A closed container is a U shape: two vertical side bars with a continuous\n  bottom segment between them and an empty rectangular interior above the bottom.\n\nRule\n- Remove the top marker from its original cell.\n- If exactly one closed U-shaped container has the marker column within its\n  horizontal footprint, fill that container's empty interior with the marker color.\n- If no closed container is under the marker column, fill the whole bottom row\n  with the marker color.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker does not identify a color by value; it identifies a vertical drop\n  column, and only a structurally closed U shape can catch and contain the fill.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2d0172a1\" lines=\"209-224\">\n<description>\nConvert a rough nested line drawing into a clean canonical frame-and-dot diagram.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one background color and one foreground color.\n- Foreground 4-connected objects are rough closed frames or small filled blobs.\n- Frames may contain other frames/blobs; blobs outside a frame remain outside it.\n\nRule:\n- Detect the containment tree of foreground objects using holes of each frame object.\n- Replace every blob with one foreground pixel.\n- Replace every frame with a rectangular border padded two cells around its direct children.\n- Place siblings by their original row/column overlap and order, with one empty cell between canonical objects.\n\nKey insight: The exact wobbly geometry is irrelevant; only enclosure plus relative sibling positions define the output.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"42f83767\" lines=\"23-39\">\n<description>\nExpand the lower color grid by replacing each pixel with its matching top stencil.\n\nInput structure\nThe input has a top legend and a lower dense color grid, separated by blank rows.\nThe legend contains left-to-right 2x2 color swatches and, to their right,\nthe same number of square neutral-color stencil masks.\n\nRule\nPair the swatches and stencil masks by left-to-right order. For every pixel in\nthe lower color grid, paste the paired stencil mask as one output block,\nrecoloring the mask pixels with that pixel's color and leaving mask holes as bg.\n\nKey insight\nThe lower grid is not copied directly; it is an index grid whose colors select\nthe stencil shape from the legend, so each input pixel becomes one square block.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"522fdd07\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nCycle each solid odd-sided square to the next smaller centered square, wrapping a single pixel to a 9x9 square.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- Every foreground object is a separated 4-connected monochrome solid square.\n- Square side lengths are odd and belong to the cycle 1, 3, 5, 7, 9.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the canvas to the background.\n- Keep each square's color and center.\n- Replace side length n by n - 2 when n > 1; replace side length 1 by side length 9.\n\nKey insight:\n- The visible operation is on square size, not color: side lengths cycle 1 -> 9 -> 7 -> 5 -> 3 -> 1.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5adee1b2\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nUse the left-edge color legend to draw a padded rectangular halo around each matching object while preserving enclosed holes.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a dominant background color.\n- One or more two-row markers sit on the left edge; each marker is a 2x2 block whose left column is an object color and right column is its halo color.\n- Away from the markers, every source color from the legend appears as one or more 4-connected monochrome objects.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the input pixels unchanged.\n- For each source-color object, take its tight bbox expanded by one cell and clipped to the grid.\n- Recolor background pixels in that expanded region to the marker's halo color, except for background holes enclosed by the object.\n\nKey insight:\n- The small left-edge bars are a legend: they map object colors to the colors used for the surrounding padded bbox.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"221dfab4\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nPropagate a repeating striped pattern from a yellow border segment toward the opposite edge, recoloring foreground objects on certain stripes.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Foreground blobs of another color scattered in the interior.\n- A short yellow (4) segment sits on one border edge.\n\nRule:\n- From the yellow segment, march toward the opposite edge in steps of 2 cells, cycling colors Y, Y, G (4, 4, 3). The 1-cell gap between stripes is forced to bg.\n- Yellow stripes paint only within the segment's span.\n- Green stripes additionally recolor all non-bg pixels across the entire row/column to green.\n\nKey insight: Green stripes act as a \"flood\" that recolors existing objects on the full row/column, while yellow stripes are confined to the segment span.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"494ef9d7\" lines=\"16-30\">\n<description>\nSlide each blue/teal or yellow/orange row pair together while preserving its left-to-right order.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a black background.\n- Each nonempty row contains exactly two distinct foreground pixels.\n- Blue/teal and yellow/orange row pairs are the moving pairs; other color pairs are distractors.\n\nRule\n- For each moving pair, keep the left pixel fixed and move the right pixel left until it touches the left pixel.\n- Clear the moved pixel's original location, and leave non-moving rows unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- Only same-hue-family pixel pairs attract horizontally; separated unrelated colors do not compact.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8e5c0c38\" lines=\"9-21\">\n<description>\nClean each color layer by enforcing vertical mirror symmetry.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Several non-bg colors, each forming a nearly-symmetric pattern about some vertical axis.\n- Some pixels in each color are \"noise\" that breaks the symmetry.\n\nRule:\n- For each non-bg color, find the vertical axis that best explains its pixel layout.\n- Remove any pixel that lacks a same-color mirror counterpart across that axis.\n\nKey insight: Each color has its own independent symmetry axis; the task is per-color denoising, not whole-grid symmetry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e734a0e8\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nCopy the unique colored sprite tile into every tiled cell marked by an internal separator-colored pixel.\n\nInput structure\n- Full same-color separator rows and columns partition the grid into equal rectangular tiles.\n- One tile contains the only non-background, non-separator colored sprite.\n- Target tiles are otherwise background-colored but contain at least one internal pixel of the separator color.\n\nRule\n- Keep the separator lines and the source sprite tile unchanged.\n- Replace each marked target tile with an exact copy of the source tile.\n- Unmarked background-only tiles remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The internal separator-colored pixels are placement markers; the single colored tile is the template to stamp into those marked cells.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
